We share the answers to our homework with a friend who asks to see our work. We don't push back when a colleague suggests bending the rules on a time sheet. We agree to keep a friend's infidelity a secret, even when it makes us uncomfortable. Listening to Vanessa made me realize why there is a vast gulf between what we predict we might do and what we actually do when we are confronted with problematic behavior. We look as outsiders at the situation and we say, why would you tolerate this?
我们把家庭作业的答案分享给要求查看我们作业的朋友。当同事建议在工时表上变通规则时，我们不会进行回击。我们同意为朋友的不忠行为保密，即使这让我们感到不舒服。听了凡妮莎的话，我意识到为什么在我们预测自己可能会做什么与面对问题行为时实际会做什么之间，存在着巨大的鸿沟。我们作为局外人审视这种情况，然后会说：“你为什么要容忍这种事？”

79
00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:36.000
Why wouldn't you just stand up and say, I'm not going to do this, I'm not going to hurt another person? I need to be able to do my job and you're affecting my ability to do my job. But in fact, the social pressure, the concern about offending another person, the social anxiety in that situation is so palpable to that individual that it feels almost impossible for them to stand up and say something about it
“你为什么不直接站出来说，‘我不会这么做，我不会伤害另一个人？我需要能够完成我的工作，而你正在影响我完成工作能力’？”但事实上，社会压力、对冒犯他人的担忧、那种情境下的社交焦虑对那个人来说是如此真切，以至于他们觉得几乎不可能站出来说些什么

80
00:38:36.000 --> 00:39:11.000
and reject the sort of behavior that they're encountering. Hidden brain listener Anna Aburuz called in with a story that illustrates how egocentric bias can affect workplace behavior. She was training to be an air traffic controller and saw examples of bullying and harassing behavior all around her. She says the trainers had a clear message for trainees. Don't be soft or you know you got to have thick skin to survive in air traffic. That was a common one for sure. You have to have thick skin to survive in air traffic.
并拒绝他们所遭遇的那种行为。《隐藏的大脑》听众安娜·阿布鲁兹（Anna Aburuz）打来电话，讲述了一个说明自我中心偏见如何影响工作场所行为的故事。她当时正在接受空中交通管制员的培训，周围到处都是霸凌和骚扰行为的例子。她说，培训师对学员有一个明确的信息：“不要表现得软弱，你知道，要想在航空管制领域生存下去，你必须脸皮厚。”这绝对是常见的一句。你必须脸皮厚才能在航空管制领域生存下来。

81
00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:56.000
I've heard that over a hundred times. Anna recalled one painful incident. There was a trainee that was trying to clear an aircraft for landing. And the trainer in that moment grabbed the headset of the trainee and this headset is plugged in to the radar and they grabbed that headset and they threw it across the room which would fly off of the head of the trainee. They would actually tell them, hey hurry up and go grab it so that you can plug back in and clear this aircraft for landing. Anna says seeing such incidents made her fearful. She didn't feel she could complain, since
这样的话我听过不下上百次。安娜回忆起了一起痛苦的事件。当时有一名学员正试图指挥一架飞机降落。那一刻，培训师抓住了学员的耳机——这个耳机是连在雷达上的——他们抓起那个耳机，把它扔过了房间，耳机直接从学员的头上飞了出去。他们甚至会对他们说：“嘿，快点去捡回来，这样你才能重新插上并指挥这架飞机降落。”安娜说，看到这样的事件让她感到恐惧。她觉得她不能抱怨，因为

82
00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:47.000
such behaviors appear to be the norm. Who could she complain to? The people who were themselves acting badly. They would just say things like what are you doing if you do that again I swear to God. One time when she was directing aircraft this was in real life not a simulation she found herself sitting next to one of the trainers who she says had acted abusively to our trainees. By this point Anna was no longer a trainee. She was directing two aircraft. One was a thousand feet above the other. I got the names of the of the aircraft mixed up and I because I was so nervous I think and I descended the wrong aircraft. I descended the one on top instead of the one on the bottom because
这些行为似乎已经成了常态。她能向谁抱怨呢？那些本身行为就很恶劣的人。他们只会说类似这样的话：“你在干什么？如果你再这样做，我向天发誓……”有一次，当她在指挥飞机时——这是在现实生活中，而不是模拟器中——她发现自己坐在其中一位培训师旁边，据她说，这位培训师曾对学员有虐待行为。此时的安娜已经不再是学员了。她在指挥两架飞机。一架在另一架上方一千英尺。我把这两架飞机的代号弄混了，因为我太紧张了，我想，我让错误的飞机下降了。我让上面那架飞机下降了，而不是下面那架，因为

83
00:40:47.000 --> 00:41:19.000
I got those call signs messed up. She told the aircraft that was at a higher altitude to descend directly into the path of the lower altitude aircraft. Luckily the pilot could see the aircraft so the pilot was just, no, we're not going to descend. And I immediately knew what I had just done. And I thought, today was a clear day. It was clear skies. There was no clouds in the way. There wasn't any storm clouds in the way. But had there been storm clouds, or had there
我把那些呼号搞混了。她告诉飞行高度较高的飞机直接下降，飞入飞行高度较低的飞机的航线上。幸运的是，飞行员能看到那架飞机，所以飞行员直接说：“不，我们不下降。”我立刻意识到我刚刚做了什么。我想，那天是个好天气。天高云淡。没有云彩挡路。也没有任何暴风雨云挡路。但如果有暴风雨云，或者有

84
00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:43.000
been some other kind of visual obstruction, this plane would have descended and they would have hit that aircraft and I would have been responsible for hundreds of deaths. And it wasn't because I didn't know how to control the traffic, I did. And I had done this a million times. It was because of the social stress that I was in at that time
其他某种视觉障碍，这架飞机就会下降并撞上那架飞机，而我将为数百人的死亡负责。这不是因为我不知道如何管制交通，我懂得。而且我已经这样做过无数次了。这是因为当时我所处的社会压力

85
00:41:43.000 --> 00:42:28.000
that didn't make me think clearly. I told Vanessa Barnes what Anna described, how the mere presence of the trainer had disrupted her to the point where she made a mistake that could have been catastrophic. Vanessa said, look, it's certainly the case that there are lots of unethical people who know they are unethical and lots of bullies who know they are bullies. Maybe that was the case here. But there is a deeper problem in the workplace that we often forget. The bullies and harassers who don't know that they are bullies and harassers. Often, when we're the person causing someone else distress, we can't see that distress. It's invisible to us.
让我无法清晰地思考。我把安娜所描述的情况告诉了凡妮莎·邦兹：培训师的存在是如何干扰她到让她犯下可能造成灾难性后果的错误。凡妮莎说，看，当然有很多不道德的人知道自己不道德，也有很多恶霸知道自己是恶霸。也许这里的情况就是这样。但在工作场所中，有一个我们经常忘记的更深层次的问题。那些不知道自己是恶霸和骚扰者的恶霸和骚扰者。通常，当我们是给他人带来痛苦的人时，我们却看不到这种痛苦。这对我们来说是隐形的。

86
00:42:28.000 --> 00:43:10.000
And it's not to let anybody off the hook, because clearly it's the people creating this toxic culture's responsibility to kind of fix it and to not cause these things to happen. But there's also this cognitive bias there where we may not realize the extent to which we're interfering with somebody else's performance. These same dynamics play out in another common occurrence in the workplace, unwanted romantic attention. We ran a couple of studies where we asked people about their experiences being asked out at work or asking someone out at work. And we ask people to imagine situations where they weren't interested in the other person or the other person wasn't interested in them.
这并不是为了给任何人开脱，因为显然修复这种有毒文化、不让这些事情发生，是创造这种文化的人的责任。但那里也存在这种认知偏差，我们可能没有意识到自己在多大程度上干扰了别人的表现。同样的动态也在工作场所的另一个常见现象中上演——不受欢迎的浪漫关注。我们进行了一些研究，询问人们在工作中被邀约或在工作约别人的经历。我们让人们想象那些他们对对方不感兴趣，或者对方对他们不感兴趣的情境。

87
00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:37.000
And what we found is that people who asked somebody out at work and were rejected thought that it was pretty easy for that person to reject them, right? They didn't think that that person experienced a whole lot of distress and they didn't think that they changed their behavior very much after being asked out. But when people recalled situations where they were asked out by someone at work who they weren't interested in, they described feeling obligated to say yes, feeling much
我们发现，在工作中约了别人却被拒绝的人认为，对方拒绝他们其实挺容易的，对吧？他们不认为对方经历了很多痛苦，也不认为对方在被邀约后改变了多少行为。但当人们回忆起在工作中被自己不感兴趣的人约过的情景时，他们形容自己感到了说“好的”义务，对向对方说“不”感到

88
00:43:37.000 --> 00:44:05.000
more uncomfortable saying no to the person, and they reported doing all sorts of things to try to avoid that person that the other person didn't realize that they were doing. So in fact, this little request, we tell people to just go for it and ask this person out. It actually puts a lot more pressure on the other person than we tend to realize when we're the ones doing the asking. In some ways, we underestimate the pressure that we exert on other people. In some ways, that's the moral of the whole story here, isn't it?
更加不舒服，并且他们报告说自己做了各种各样的尝试来避开那个人，而对方根本没有意识到他们在这么做。因此，事实上，这个小小的请求——我们告诉人们勇敢去约这个人吧——实际上给对方施加了比我们作为发起者所意识到的要大得多的压力。在某些方面，我们低估了我们对他人施加的压力。从某种意义上说，这就是整个故事的寓意，不是吗？

89
00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:48.000
Absolutely, yeah. We underestimate the influence that we have over other people, and we underestimate the extent to which asking them for something really puts them in an awkward position, because now they have to say no, and that's just a really hard thing for people to do. Like many psychological biases, the tendency we have to downplay the influence we have on others can have far-reaching consequences. It can keep us from asking for help that would be forthcoming. It can keep us from reaching out and making friends with strangers. And it can also lead us to give in to unethical demands or make improper demands of other people. I asked Vanessa how her research had prompted her
绝对是的。我们低估了我们对其他人的影响力，并且我们低估了向他们索取东西确实把他们置于一个尴尬境地的程度，因为现在他们必须说“不”，而这对于人们来说真的是一件很难做到的事情。就像许多心理偏差一样，我们淡化对他人影响力的倾向会产生深远的影响。它会阻止我们寻求本可以得到的帮助。它会阻止我们主动与陌生人交往并结交朋友。它还会导致我们向不道德的要求妥协，或者对他人提出不当的要求。我问凡妮莎她的研究是如何促使她

