00:44:48.000 --> 00:45:12.000
to do things differently in her own life. It has made a huge difference in the little things. So for example, when I was pregnant, if I needed a seat on the subway or on a train, I would kind of stand there and look around and try to look my most pathetic so that someone would give me their seat, thinking that someone would step up and do it because they were nice, right?
在自己的生活中做出改变的。它在生活中的小事上带来了巨大的改变。例如，当我怀孕时，如果我需要在地铁或火车上找个座位，我就会站在那里四处张望，尽量让自己看起来最可怜，好让别人把座位让给我，心想别人会因为善良而主动站出来让座，对吧？

91
00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:30.000
But in fact, everyone's all involved in their own stuff. They're not necessarily looking around and paying attention. And maybe they'd be perfectly happy to give up their seat, but they're not going to think of it unless you actually ask. And so I tried to take that into account. So when I was pregnant, I would go up to people and be like, hey, can I sit down? I'd really love to sit.
但事实上，每个人都沉浸在自己的事情中。他们不一定在环顾四周并留意这些。也许他们非常乐意让出座位，但除非你真的开口要求，否则他们不会想到这一点。所以，我试着把这一点考虑进去。因此，当我怀孕时，我会走到别人面前说：“嘿，我能坐一下吗？我很想坐一会儿。”

92
00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:46.000
And then, of course, people are incredibly happy to just pop up and say yes. And what's interesting, of course, is when that happens, you're actually giving people an opportunity to do something nice. It's not just that you're imposing on them. Presumably, some of them are actually happy to say, I was just writing to work and now I actually got to do this nice thing
然后，当然，人们会非常乐意地一下子站起来说“好”。而有趣的是，当然，当这种情况发生时，你实际上是给了人们一个做好事的机会。这不仅仅是你强加于他们。据推测，他们中的一些人实际上很高兴地说：“我只是在通勤上班，现在我居然能为另一个人做这件好事。”

93
00:45:46.000 --> 00:46:03.000
for this other person. I feel this little warm glow. Yeah, absolutely. So a lot of people wonder about the takeaway. So if people agree to help us out of obligation because they feel like they can't say no, then do you really want to ask them for things? But people are really good at justifying their behaviors
我感到了微微的暖意。是的，绝对是这样。所以很多人都在想结论是什么。如果人们因为觉得自己无法拒绝而出于义务同意帮忙，那你真的想向他们寻求帮助吗？但人们非常擅长为自己的行为辩护，

94
00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:17.000
and ways that make them feel good about themselves. So they may agree to help because they feel like they can't say no, but pretty quickly after that, they're going to be convincing themselves that they helped because they're a really wonderful person. And so everyone's going to walk away feeling good about the interaction.
用能让他们对自己感觉良好的方式。因此，他们可能会因为觉得自己无法拒绝而同意帮忙，但在那之后不久，他们就会说服自己，他们帮忙是因为他们是个非常棒的人。于是每个人都会带着对这次互动的美好感觉离开。

95
00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:55.000
You got the help that you needed and the other person gets to feel like a good person. Psychologists once conducted a light-hearted version of Stanley Milgram's Obedience Study. In the 1970s, they had research assistants stand on the streets of New York City. Their jobs? To look skyward. At nothing. The question the researchers wanted to know was whether innocent passers-by would also stop to look up to see what was going on. They found that when more people were in on the gag, more pedestrians stopped and looked up.
你得到了你需要的帮助，而另一个人也得以觉得自己是个好人。心理学家曾经进行过一个轻松版本的斯坦利·米尔格拉姆服从实验。在 20 世纪 70 年代，他们让研究助理站在纽约市的街头。他们的工作是什么？抬头望向天空。看着虚无。研究人员想要探究的问题是，无辜的过路人是否也会停下来抬头看看究竟发生了什么。他们发现，当更多的人加入这个恶作剧时，更多的行人停了下来并抬头张望。

96
00:46:55.000 --> 00:47:46.000
I've seen video of that study many times and always found this scene funny. One to fifteen people just staring off into the sky. Recently I rewatched it and this time I did what Vanessa had done. I flipped the script. Instead of seeing the experiment from the point of view of the passersby and asking myself whether I would be similarly influenced, I looked at the experiment from the point of view of the research assistants. Did they expect so many people to join them in looking at nothing? We've seen throughout this episode how all of us as individuals have great power to shape how others behave. If each of us has this hidden power, then collectively, as groups, as communities, as tribes,
我看过那个研究的视频很多次，并且一直觉得这个场景很搞笑。一十五个人只是盯着天空发呆。最近我重温了一遍，这次我做了凡妮莎做过的事。我颠覆了剧本。我没有从路人的角度来看待这个实验，也没有问自己是否会受到同样的启发，而是从研究助理的角度来看待这个实验。他们曾预料到会有这么多人加入他们一起仰望虚空吗？我们在这一集的节目中看到，我们作为个人，都拥有巨大的力量来塑造他人的行为。如果我们每个人都拥有这种隐藏的力量，那么作为一个集体、作为社区、作为群体，

97
00:47:46.000 --> 00:48:37.000
we are going to have even more influence. How we choose to use that influence? That's up to each of us. HIDDEN BRAIN is produced by Hidden Brain Media. Our audio production team includes Annie Murphy Paul, Kristen Wong, Laura Quarell, Ryan Katz, Autumn Barnes, Andrew Chadwick and Nick Woodbury. Tara Boyle is our executive producer. I'm Hidden Brain's executive editor. Tara Boyle is our executive producer. I'm Hidden Brains executive editor. We end today with a story from our sister show, My Unsung Hero. It's brought to you by T-Mobile for Business. Our story comes from Bethany Renfri.
